  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION/MARINE/BEACHES DISCUSSION...\n\nMid level water vapor imagery shows high pressure firmly in place \nover the Four Corners, with ongoing heat impacts for Southern \nCalifornia. Highs both today and tomorrow will be around 3-7 degrees \nabove normal with warm overnight lows 4-10 degrees above normal, \nresulting in minor to moderate HeatRisk for inland and valley areas \nand moderate to major HeatRisk for the deserts. A Heat Advisory \nand Extreme Heat Warning remain in effect through Sunday evening.\n\nOutside of the heat, attention returns to monsoonal moisture as the \nupper level ridge will elongate and stretch this coming week, \nallowing for multiple weak waves to move across. Forcing this \nafternoon looks limited with lackluster low-level moisture for areas \nother than the southern mountains. If any storms manage to develop, \nthey will likely be confined to the higher terrain this afternoon \nwith limited impacts. The ridge will become a bit more compressed \ntonight and, as seen Friday morning, another very weak easterly wave \nmay be able to move across Baja late tonight. Localized light \nrainfall will possible in San Diego County Sunday morning, \nalthough the best chances for any early morning precipitation will\nbe in the San Diego County Mountains and foothills, or eastern \nI-8 corridor. This window will also allow for low and mid level \nmoisture to begin to surge further northward, reaching Riverside \nand San Bernardino Counties by early Sunday afternoon as PW values\nreturn above 1.15. While not sensational, this, along with the \nslight breakdown in the ridge, may allow for a few decent \nthunderstorms to develop over the mountains, especially in \nRiverside and San Diego counties, Sunday afternoon (30-40% \nchance). Brief, heavy rainfall with rain rates up to \n0.50-0.75\"/hr and lightning will be the main threats. There will \nbe around a 10-20% chance for a storm to fire off of the San \nBernardino Mountains around this time too, but rain rates that far\nnorth will likely be 0.40\"/hr or less. If you are spending time \nin the mountains this weekend, ensure you are prepared to take \nshelter in a sturdy, enclosed structure should you find yourself \ncaught in a thunderstorm. Trees are not a safe place to shelter \nfrom lightning!\n\nThe pattern shifts cooler beginning Monday as high pressure slides \neast, with a deepening marine layer into the end of the week. The \nposition of the high centered over the South Plains and a separate \nhigh off the coast will establish upper level troughing for much \nof CA as we cool back down to near normal for mid August by \nTuesday. Monday and Tuesday will once again feature afternoon \nmonsoonal convection in the mountains as moisture is pulled \nnorthward and PWs reach ~1.5. Both of these days could see more \nwidespread storms and higher rain rates, with a slightly higher \npotential for storms to move off the mountains and into the \ndeserts compared to Sunday. Afternoon storms over the mountains \nwill be possible each day through the end of next week, but \nchances decrease after Tuesday as a more robust trough takes \neffect. \n\nYou've been patient, and the cooler weather is finally in view!",
